      </div>

      <h2>5. Dearness Allowance on Transport Allowance (DA on TA)</h2>
      <p>
        Many employees overlook that Dearness Allowance applies to more than just base salary. Under Central Civil Services (Allowance) Rules, employees are entitled to draw <strong>Dearness Allowance on Transport Allowance</strong> to offset rising commuting and fuel costs.
      </p>

      <div class="formula-banner">
        Total Transport Allowance = Base TA Rate + [Base TA Rate × (Current DA % ÷ 100)]
      </div>

      <p>
        For instance, an officer in Level 7 stationed in a Class X metro receives a base Transport Allowance of ₹3,600 per month. When DA stands at 53%, the employee receives:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Base TA = ₹3,600</li>
        <li>DA on TA (53% of ₹3,600) = ₹1,908</li>
        <li><strong>Total Disbursed Transport Allowance = ₹5,508 per month</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When a new Pay Commission resets DA to 0%, the "DA on TA" component temporarily drops to zero, and the employee draws only the baseline TA until fresh DA installments accumulate.
      </p>

      <h2>6. The 25% and 50% DA Milestones: Automated Allowance Triggers</h2>
      <p>
        Under the 7th CPC framework, crossing specific DA thresholds triggered automatic increases in other compensatory allowances without requiring separate cabinet interventions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The 25% DA Threshold:</strong> Automatically raised House Rent Allowance (HRA) rates from initial baseline percentages (24%, 16%, and 8%) to <strong>27%, 18%, and 9%</strong> across Class X, Y, and Z cities.</li>
        <li><strong>The 50% DA Threshold:</strong> Fully restored HRA rates to their historical ceilings of <strong>30%, 20%, and 10%</strong>. Crossing 50% DA also triggered an automatic 25% increase across multiple allowances:
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Children Education Allowance (CEA):</strong> Scaled from ₹2,250 to ₹2,812.50 per month per child.</li>
            <li><strong>Hostel Subsidy:</strong> Scaled from ₹6,750 to ₹8,437.50 per month.</li>
            <li><strong>Retirement Gratuity Ceiling:</strong> Increased by 25%, expanding the statutory ceiling from ₹20 Lakhs to ₹25 Lakhs.</li>
            <li><strong>Special Duty Allowance & Child Care Allowance:</strong> Increased automatically by 25%.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>7. The 8th Pay Commission Reset: Why DA Returns to Zero Percent</h2>
      <p>
        Whenever a new Central Pay Commission takes effect, the accumulated Dearness Allowance counter is set back to <strong>0%</strong>. This is a standard part of pay consolidation, not a reduction in earnings:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Merger into Basic Pay:</strong> Over the 7th CPC cycle, Dearness Allowance surpassed 50%. Under the 8th Pay Commission, this accumulated inflation cushion is permanently integrated into basic pay via the approved fitment factor (e.g., 2.25x to 2.86x).</li>
        <li><strong>Establishing the New Base:</strong> Because the basic salary expands to absorb all prior inflation, it becomes the new starting baseline for future cost-of-living adjustments.</li>
        <li><strong>Starting Fresh:</strong> With the baseline recalibrated, the DA counter resets to <strong>0%</strong> on the effective rollout date. Future semi-annual installments then accumulate from zero, compounding on top of the enlarged basic pay.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>8. Tax Treatment of Dearness Allowance</h2>
      <p>
        Under Section 17(1) of the Income Tax Act, 1961, Dearness Allowance is classified as income from salary and is <strong>100% taxable</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No Special Exemptions:</strong> Unlike House Rent Allowance (which offers exemptions under Section 10(13A)) or Transport Allowance (which offers exemptions for employees with disabilities), DA enjoys no statutory deductions.</li>
        <li><strong>Tax Withholding (TDS):</strong> Drawing and Disbursing Officers (DDOs) include full DA earnings when computing monthly tax deductions (TDS) under both the Old and New Tax Regimes.</li>
        <li><strong>Pensioners (Dearness Relief):</strong> DR disbursed to central retirees is equally treated as taxable pension income under Section 192.</li>
      </ul>

